module.exports = [

{ order: 1, title: "What sociological enquiry is for: questions, evidence and the Unit 2 map",
content: `
<p>Sociology earns its place as a science not by its subject matter but by how it handles evidence. This unit is where the discipline's craft lives: how a question becomes a hypothesis, a hypothesis becomes a design, a design becomes data, and data become findings that other sociologists can check. Lesson 7 used the evidence base of the Youth Cultures option — official statistics, self-report studies, victim surveys — and repeatedly stopped to ask how good the numbers were. This chapter teaches you to ask that question systematically, because in AS Unit 2 you are examined on the method, not just the findings.</p>
<p>Four distinctions organise everything that follows, and each is a pair of questions about the <em>source</em> of evidence and its <em>form</em>. <strong>Primary data</strong> are collected by the researcher for this study — your own survey, your own interviews, your own observations. <strong>Secondary data</strong> already exist, collected by someone else for another purpose — official statistics, government reports, other sociologists' studies, newspaper archives, personal documents. <strong>Quantitative data</strong> are numerical: counts, rates, percentages, scores. <strong>Qualitative data</strong> are meaningful: words, transcripts, field notes, images. Neither column of either pair is superior in itself — a secondary official statistic can outperform a badly designed primary survey, and a handful of vivid interviews can outmeasure a thousand sloppy questionnaires — but each combination buys something and costs something, and Unit 2 is largely the study of those prices.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The four distinctions to memorise:</strong> primary (collected by the researcher for this study) vs secondary (already existing, collected for another purpose); quantitative (numerical) vs qualitative (words, meanings, images). Every method you meet in Lessons 9 and 10 is a cell in this 2×2 map — and most studies now deliberately mix cells.</p>
</div>
<p>The unit has a route, and it is this lesson's route. First, <strong>planning</strong>: turning a question into a testable design — aims, hypotheses, variables, operationalisation, sampling and ethics (pages 2–12). Second, <strong>collecting</strong>: the quantitative toolkit (Lesson 9) and the qualitative toolkit (Lesson 10). Third, <strong>evaluating</strong>: the vocabulary of reliability, validity, representativeness and generalisability, and the WJEC research-synopsis exercises that test it (Lesson 11). Each lesson links to a live research context — education, media or religion — because WJEC examines methods <em>applied</em>, not recited: you must be able to say not only what a stratified sample is but how you would build one of a school's pupils.</p>
<p>One warning before the detail, taken from Lesson 7's own evidence: data are never raw. A conviction rate is the joint product of behaviour and policing; a survey answer is the joint product of opinion and the question's wording; an interview is the joint product of the respondent's world and the researcher's presence. The methods course exists because every number and every quotation has a biography — and the mark scheme rewards the sociologist who can tell it.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Distinguish between primary and secondary data, giving one example of each. (b) Distinguish between quantitative and qualitative data, and explain why neither is simply "better". (c) Explain what it means to say data are "never raw", using one example from Lesson 7.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Primary data are collected by the researcher for the study in hand — for example a questionnaire the researcher administers to pupils; secondary data already exist, collected by someone else for another purpose — for example Ministry of Justice proven-offending statistics, or another sociologist's published study. (b) Quantitative data are numerical (counts, rates, scores) and allow comparison and pattern-spotting across large numbers; qualitative data are verbal or visual (transcripts, field notes) and capture meaning from the inside; neither is simply better because they answer different questions — how many/who/where against what does it mean — and the cost of each is the other's strength (quantitative breadth sacrifices meaning; qualitative depth sacrifices scale and replicability). (c) It means data are produced by a process whose decisions shape them: the age–crime curve of Lesson 7 is not raw behaviour but "proven offending", jointly produced by what young people do and by what the police look for, whom they stop and what courts accept — so before treating any figure as evidence, the sociologist must ask how it was made.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 2, title: "Aims, hypotheses and variables: turning a question into something testable",
content: `
<p>A <strong>research aim</strong> states what the study wants to find out, in one sentence: to investigate the relationship between part-time employment and sixth-form attainment. An aim is not yet researchable, because it names concepts but no measures. The bridge from aim to design runs through the <strong>hypothesis</strong>: a precise, testable statement of the relationship the researcher expects between the <strong>variables</strong> — the things that vary — the study will compare.</p>
<p>Every hypothesis names two roles. The <strong>independent variable</strong> (IV) is what the researcher treats as the possible cause or prior condition; the <strong>dependent variable</strong> (DV) is what might change in response. In "the more hours a sixth-form student works in a part-time job, the lower their average grade", hours worked is the IV and average grade the DV. Note the grammar of the sentence: it is the hypothesis form sociologists actually use — a statement about the <em>relationship</em> between variables. When the researcher predicts a direction ("lower", "higher"), the hypothesis is <strong>directional</strong>; when it predicts only that a relationship or difference exists, without saying which way, it is <strong>non-directional</strong> ("there is a relationship between hours worked and average grade"). The choice matters: a directional prediction is riskier and so more informative if supported, but a non-directional hypothesis is the honest form when previous research gives no clear basis for expecting one direction over another.</p>
<p>The deepest distinction is between <strong>causal</strong> and <strong>correlational</strong> hypotheses. A causal claim says the IV changes the DV: reducing class size causes attainment to rise. A correlational claim says only that the two vary together: schools with smaller classes also tend to have higher attainment — which could be because smaller classes raise attainment, because advantaged families choose such schools, or because of something else entirely. Outside the laboratory, sociology is overwhelmingly correlational: we cannot randomly assign children to schools or families to poverty, so the honest hypothesis is usually the correlational one, and the temptation to upgrade it into a causal sentence is the most common way a findings section overreaches. Lesson 11 returns to this as the correlation–causation trap.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A good hypothesis is:</strong> a clear statement of an expected relationship; built from variables that will be <em>operationalised</em> (page 3); testable against evidence that could contradict it; and, wherever the evidence allows, directional. "Investigating young people and religion" is an aim; "the proportion of 16- to 24-year-olds describing themselves as religious falls with age of cohort" is a hypothesis.</p>
</div>
<p>Finally, hypotheses belong to the positivist half of the discipline (page 4): they are the instruments of a science that predicts and tests. An interpretivist study begins instead with <strong>research questions</strong> — open, meaning-first: what does "being religious" mean to sixth-formers who rarely attend? Both starting points are legitimate; what is not legitimate is dressing one up as the other. A study that claims to test a hypothesis with no measureable variables, or one that claims to have "explored meanings" while counting ticks, loses marks in the synopsis exercises precisely for this mismatch.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Write a directional and a non-directional hypothesis for the aim "to investigate the relationship between school absence and GCSE attainment". (b) Identify the IV and DV in each. (c) Explain why most sociological hypotheses are correlational rather than causal. (d) Why does an interpretivist study begin with research questions instead of hypotheses?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Directional: "the more days a pupil is absent in Year 11, the lower their total GCSE points score"; non-directional: "there is a relationship between days absent in Year 11 and total GCSE points score". (b) IV = days absent (the prior condition treated as the possible cause); DV = total GCSE points score (what may change in response) — in both versions. (c) Because a causal claim requires the researcher to control the IV — to assign cases to levels of it and rule out alternatives — and sociology mostly cannot: children cannot be randomly assigned to absence, families or schools, so the researcher observes variables that vary together and cannot exclude other causes (family circumstances, health, school quality); the honest form is therefore "varies with", not "causes". (d) Because the interpretivist aim is understanding meanings from the actor's own point of view (Verstehen), which cannot be predicted in advance as a relationship between measured variables: open questions let the categories and definitions emerge in the field, whereas a hypothesis fixes the answer's shape before the meanings have even been heard.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 3, title: "Operationalisation: from concepts to indicators to measures",
content: `
<p>Between the hypothesis and the questionnaire item lies the least glamorous and most examinable step in the whole research cycle: <strong>operationalisation</strong> — specifying exactly how each concept in the hypothesis will be recognised and measured in the field. A concept like "class", "religiosity", "achievement" or "family" is a shared idea, useful precisely because it is general. A measure is narrow, concrete and countable. The whole craft of design is walking from one to the other without losing the concept on the way.</p>
<p>Take "social class". The <strong>concept</strong> is a person's general social and economic position. To study it, a researcher must choose an <strong>indicator</strong> — a visible feature that stands for the concept — and then a <strong>measure</strong>: the actual question or record. Occupation has been the classic British indicator, and the measure could be the Office for National Statistics' socio-economic classification (NS-SEC) applied to the respondent's job title. Alternatively the indicator could be household income (measured in bands), education level, or housing tenure — four different indicators, four different measures, all called "class", and all yielding different patterns. Durkheim's Suicide (1897) made the move famous: "integration" — a concept nobody can see — was indicated by marital status, religion and family size, measured from official records, and compared across regions of nineteenth-century Europe.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> "operationalisation is just paperwork before the real work begins." It is the opposite: it is where validity is won or lost. If "religiosity" is operationalised as church attendance, the study will find teenagers irreligious — even if many hold strong personal beliefs, because attendance was never the same thing as the concept. The gap between the indicator and the concept is the study's central validity threat, and it is decided here, not in the analysis.</p>
</div>
<p>Religion — one of WJEC's live contexts — shows the choice at its sharpest. If the aim is to chart change in institutional religion in Britain, attendance and membership are defensible indicators: they measure the public, collective dimension of religion the aim is about. If the aim is what religion means to young people, the same measures are a category error: Grace Davie's phrase "believing without belonging" names exactly the pattern such an operationalisation would miss. The study of religion therefore teaches the rule for every study: <strong>the indicator must match the definition</strong>. First define the concept ("religion as participation in collective practice" or "religion as personal belief"), then choose the indicator that measures that definition and no other.</p>
<p>Operationalisation also fixes the study's comparability. Two studies of "pupil achievement" that operationalise it as "5+ GCSEs at grade 4 or above" and as "teacher-assessed effort" cannot have their findings compared or combined, whatever their titles say. This is why successive governments have standardised measures (the English Baccalaureate, NS-SEC, the census categories): standard operationalisations make evidence cumulative — and why changing them (reformed GCSE grading, census questions on ethnicity) always breaks the trend line and produces apparent jumps that are really changes in the measure.</p>
<p>The page's work is one you will do in the synopsis exercises: given an aim, you must be able to propose a defensible operationalisation and defend it against the obvious rival. The practice questions below are that exercise in miniature.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Define operationalisation and name its three steps. (b) A researcher wants to study "the extent of religious belief among sixth-formers". Propose one indicator and one measure, and name one thing it would miss. (c) Why does changing a standard measure (for example a new GCSE grading system) damage the usefulness of official statistics? (d) Why was marital status a defensible indicator of "integration" in Durkheim's study, and what would a critic say about it?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Operationalisation is specifying exactly how a concept will be recognised and measured in the field; the steps run concept → indicator (the visible feature standing for the concept) → measure (the actual question or record that produces the data). (b) Indicator: personal belief; measure: a survey item such as "Do you believe in God or a higher power? (yes/no/unsure)" administered to a sample of sixth-formers. It would miss practised but privately-held belief expressed in action (prayer, festival observance), belief without theism (some Buddhist and humanist positions), and the distinction between nominal and salient belief — so a better design adds items on practice and salience. (c) Because statistics are only comparable if the measure is constant: a reformed grading system changes what a "pass" counts, so apparent rises or falls in attainment after the change measure the new definition rather than any change in learning — the trend line is broken at the point of redefinition. (d) Marriage rates, religion and family size were reliably recorded in official statistics across many European regions, making "integration" countable and comparable — defensible as a measure; the critic's reply is that marital status indicates a social form rather than integration itself: the unmarried may be deeply integrated in occupational or associational networks, so the indicator partly measures something else (the concept's other dimensions), which is why Durkheim triangulated across several indicators rather than one.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 4, title: "Positivism: society as measurable fact",
content: `
<p>Every design decision in the next three lessons is downstream of one argument: what kind of thing is society, and therefore what kind of evidence counts? The first answer, <strong>positivism</strong>, was sociology's founding self-image. Auguste Comte, who coined the term in the 1830s, proposed a "science of society" on the model of the natural sciences: society is a reality that exists outside and above individuals — Comte called it, and later Durkheim systematised it, a realm of <strong>social facts</strong> — and its laws can be discovered by systematic observation, measurement and comparison, exactly as physics discovers the laws of matter.</p>
<p>The consequences for method are strict and logical. If social facts are external and measurable, then: research should be <strong>quantitative</strong>, converting behaviour into numbers; instruments should be <strong>standardised</strong>, so every respondent is measured by the same yardstick; samples should be <strong>large and representative</strong>, because laws are patterns across whole populations, not portraits of individuals; and the whole procedure must be <strong>replicable</strong>, so another researcher can repeat the study and reach the same result — the test that separates a finding from an anecdote. The values this toolkit serves are the ones Unit 2 examines constantly: <strong>reliability</strong> (the same instrument, used again, produces the same result) and <strong>representativeness</strong> (the sample stands for the population from which it was drawn).</p>
<p>The classic demonstration remains Durkheim's <em>Suicide</em> (1897), met in Lesson 1 as a founding study of social facts and worth meeting again here as a piece of <em>method</em>. Suicide looks like the most private act there is — the least sociological of subjects. Durkheim made it the proof of sociology by showing that the <em>rates</em> were stable and patterned: Protestant regions had higher rates than Catholic ones; the married lower than the unmarried; rates rose and fell with economic change. If each suicide were purely individual, the aggregate would be as chaotic as the individuals. Stable patterns in official records could only mean external social forces — differing degrees of integration and regulation — acting on whole populations. He then explained the patterns with a theory of those forces, which is where his successors enter to argue (see the evaluation below).</p>
<p>Positivism's strengths deserve stating fairly before any criticism. Measurement enables <strong>comparison</strong> — across groups, regions and years — and comparison is what turns description into knowledge: "girls outperform boys at GCSE" is a measurable, checkable, repeatedly confirmed pattern, and no amount of rich interview material could substitute for it. Large samples permit <strong>generalisation</strong>: a well-drawn survey of 2,000 pupils can support statements about the year group as a whole, where forty vivid interviews cannot. Standardisation permits <strong>replication</strong>, the discipline's defence against self-deception. And quantitative patterns have civic force: the academic and socio-economic "gaps" that drive education policy exist in public view because someone measured them.</p>
<p>The criticisms are equally structural. First, the measurement problem from page 3: an instrument built in advance cannot register meanings its designer never anticipated — a survey of belief that never asks about spirituality outside religion measures its own definitions, not the world's. Second, <strong>artificiality</strong>: a questionnaire is an unnatural social situation, producing answers constructed for the researcher — respondents guess the "proper" answer (social desirability) or an opinion on the spot. Third, as Interactionist critics argued, human behaviour is not physics: people interpret their world, and the interpretation — the meaning — is precisely what the tick-box cannot carry. That last criticism is not a quibble; it founded the rival logic of enquiry on the next page.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Explain what "social facts" are and why they commit the positivist to quantitative method. (b) Summarise Durkheim's suicide study as a piece of method — what did he measure, and what did the pattern prove? (c) Outline three strengths of the positivist toolkit. (d) Explain the "measurement problem" criticism using a religious example.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Social facts are ways of acting, thinking and feeling external to the individual and coercive over them (institutions, norms, rates, categories) — they exist at the level of society, so they are collectively, not individually, real; this commits the positivist to quantitative method because measuring the social requires counting and comparing across whole populations, not interpreting single cases. (b) He measured official suicide rates across European regions and years, comparing them by religion, marital status and economic conditions; the proof was in the pattern: rates were stable and socially patterned (higher among Protestants, among the unmarried, in times of economic turbulence), and if suicide were purely individual the aggregate would vary as randomly as individuals do — so stable rates had to reflect external social forces (integration and regulation) acting on whole populations. (c) Comparison across groups and years; generalisation from large representative samples; replication — standardised instruments let other researchers repeat the study, protecting findings from error and bias. (d) The instrument records only what its designer anticipated: a survey operationalising religion as church attendance and denominational belief will register "religion falling among the young" even where young people hold strong private beliefs or practise outside institutions (Davie's "believing without belonging") — the measure's yield is mistaken for the concept itself, so the finding partly reports the operationalisation, not the world.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 5, title: "Interpretivism and Verstehen: meaning as the object of study",
content: `
<p>The second logic of enquiry begins from a different account of what society is. Max Weber argued that sociology is a science concerning itself with the <strong>interpretive understanding</strong> of social action — <strong>Verstehen</strong>. Human behaviour differs from physical behaviour in one decisive respect: people act on the <em>meanings</em> they give their situation. The same act — carrying a knife, wearing a headscarf, refusing to sing — means different things in different definitions of the situation, and until you understand the definition you do not know what the act is. An outsider can measure the frequency of an act and still entirely miss what, for the actor, the act is. Weber's own demonstration was the Protestant ethic: what explained the capitalist behaviour of ascetic Protestants was not how often they went to church but what their work meant to them — a <em>calling</em> proving election. Count the behaviour and you get a correlation; understand the meaning and you get the mechanism.</p>
<p><strong>Interpretivism</strong> is the family of approaches that operationalise this commitment. Its characteristic methods — unstructured interviews, participant observation, personal documents, case studies (all detailed in Lesson 10) — share a design logic: go to the setting; keep the instruments open; let the actor's own categories, not the researcher's schedule, organise what you hear. The value this toolkit serves is <strong>validity</strong>: an account that is true to the meanings and experiences of those studied, rather than merely consistent in its measurements. Where the positivist asks "is it reliable?", the interpretivist asks "is it true?" — and these are not the same question.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/wjec-as-sociology/wjec-soc-positivism-interpretivism.png" alt="A two-column comparison diagram. Left column, positivism (blue): starting point — society exists as external social facts that shape individuals and can be measured, as the natural sciences explain the physical world (Comte, 1838); methods preferred — social surveys with closed questions, official statistics, experiments, quantitative content analysis, standardised instruments with large samples; what it wants — reliability and representativeness, patterns across large numbers, generalisable, replicable findings; key evidence — Durkheim's Suicide (1897), comparing Protestant, Catholic and unmarried populations through official records; main limitation — it loses meaning: a tick-box records church attendance but not what attending means, and standardised instruments cannot follow up. Right column, interpretivism (green): starting point — society is made of meanings in interaction, people act on their definitions of the situation, so the task is Verstehen, understanding, not just measuring (Weber); methods preferred — unstructured interviews, participant observation, personal documents, case studies, open, flexible, naturalistic; what it wants — validity, what the behaviour means to those involved in their own categories; key evidence — Weber's Protestant ethic (1905), the meaning of a worldly calling rather than the frequency of church membership; main limitation — small samples, researcher interpretation and the interviewer effect make findings hard to replicate and generalise. Bottom band: the debate fixes your starting point, not your whole design — most studies mix, but every evaluation must say which logic a study belongs to because that decides which criticism applies." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The two logics of enquiry compared. Learn the columns as <em>linked chains</em>: starting point → methods → values → weakness — so that in an evaluation you can move from "the study used official statistics" straight to "reliable and comparable, but it cannot see the meanings behind the counts". Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The criticisms mirror positivism's, and are as structural. First, <strong>sample size and generalisation</strong>: understanding thirty drug users deeply does not license statements about drug users in general; the interpretivist must state findings as about these cases, in this setting. Second, <strong>reliability</strong>: an unstructured interview cannot be repeated by another researcher and produce the same result — the researcher is the instrument, and instruments differ. Third, <strong>the researcher effect</strong>: in any interview or observation the researcher is present, and presence changes behaviour; the account is a co-production of researcher and researched, not a window. Fourth, <strong>interpretation</strong>: the meanings in the final report passed through the researcher's own categories on the way — two ethnographers in the same youth club can write different books, and there is no replication test to referee between them.</p>
<p>Two honest closing notes. The debate is not a war you must adjudicate with a winner: it is a question of <em>fit</em> between question and method — Durkheim's question about rates genuinely needed rates; Weber's question about the calling genuinely needed meaning. And in practice the two logics mix constantly, in the deliberate combination called <strong>triangulation</strong>, which Lesson 10 develops: a survey establishes the pattern, the interviews explain it. But for WJEC, identify the dominant logic first, because the synopsis marks are awarded for applying the right criticisms to the right study.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Explain Verstehen and why it makes meaning the object of study. (b) Use Weber's Protestant ethic study to show the difference between measuring behaviour and understanding it. (c) Why is validity the interpretivist's key value, and how does it differ from reliability? (d) Give the four standard criticisms of interpretivist method.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Verstehen is interpretive understanding: sociology's task is to grasp the subjective meanings actors give their situations, because people act on meanings — the same physical act is different actions under different definitions of the situation, so meaning is what the behaviour <em>is</em>, and a science that misses it misses its object. (b) A positivist measure of ascetic Protestant regions (frequency of church attendance, membership) would correlate religion with capitalist behaviour; Weber showed the mechanism lies in the meaning: work understood as a calling proving election — the identical behaviour of working relentlessly means something different for the ascetic Protestant than for the pleasure-seeking entrepreneur, and it is that meaning that explains the pattern; the count finds the correlation, the understanding finds the cause. (c) Validity is faithfulness to what is actually going on for those studied — true in the actor's own terms; reliability is consistency of measurement — the same instrument again yielding the same result; they differ because a perfectly reliable measure can be invalid (a consistent questionnaire that consistently measures attendance instead of belief) and because interpretivists accept non-replicable but faithful accounts where positivists will not. (d) Small, unrepresentative samples that forbid generalisation; unreliable, non-replicable procedure (the researcher is the instrument); the researcher effect — presence changes the behaviour studied; and the interpretation problem — findings are filtered through the researcher's own categories, with no independent test to referee.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 6, title: "Choosing a method: practical, ethical and theoretical factors",
content: `
<p>Examiners' most common methods item is a form: "How would you investigate…?" — and the difference between a top-band answer and a mediocre one is almost never the method named. It is the <em>justification</em>. A justified choice is argued from three families of factors, and the habit of running all three in order is the single most transferable skill in Unit 2.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/wjec-as-sociology/wjec-soc-method-choice.png" alt="A four-panel diagram of the method-choice decision. Panel 1, practical factors (green): time and money — a postal questionnaire to 2,000 households costs less than a year of participant observation in one youth club; the researcher's skills and the setting's access — headteachers open schools to surveys, gang members do not; the subject matter — counting attendance suits schedules, understanding leaving home does not. Panel 2, ethical factors (red): informed consent from pupils themselves and from parents and schools where they are under 16; harm, stress and privacy — topics such as bereavement, offending or family conflict need protection, withdrawal rights and referral routes; the BSA Statement — some designs (covert work, deception) are only defensible when no alternative can reach the group. Panel 3, theoretical factors (blue): positivist aims demand reliability — standardised, replicable methods with large representative samples; interpretivist aims demand validity — flexible, naturalistic methods close to the actor's meanings; the hypothesis decides the data — a causal claim needs variables you can measure, a 'what does it mean?' question does not. Panel 4, the decision (dark): the chosen methods stated, not assumed; justified by naming the factors that decided it; acknowledged with the cost — what the choice will not be able to tell you; audited against ethics before any fieldwork begins. Arrows labelled 'weighed together' run from the three factor panels to the decision panel. Bottom band: the three interlock — the best answers show how; any 'how would you research' item is answered by working practical, ethical, theoretical, decision, limitation." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The three contexts of research design. The band's example is the one to master: covert observation may be the only practical route into a hidden group and the only valid one — and exactly the method the ethics rules restrict. The three families pull in different directions, and naming the tension is the justification. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Practical factors</strong> are the ones researchers actually feel first: time, money, skills and access. A funded team over a year can run a national survey; one student over a fortnight cannot. Access decides methods more than textbooks admit: schools have gatekeepers who admit questionnaires but are wary of filming; hidden populations (offenders, drug users) cannot be sampled from any frame at all, which is what snowball sampling (page 10) and covert observation exist for. The subject matter matters too — whether the study needs to count things across many people, or understand something few people can articulate unprompted.</p>
<p><strong>Ethical factors</strong> are not a soft constraint to mention last; on many designs they are decisive, and page 11 develops them fully. The headline: research with people must protect those people — consent, harm, privacy, anonymity — under the British Sociological Association's Statement of Ethical Practice, with research in schools (the WJEC education context) adding the wrinkle that the subjects are minors: consent must come from pupils <em>and</em> from parents and schools, and the researcher must plan what to do when a pupil discloses something safeguarding rules require passing on. An ethically impossible design is not a clever design that got caught; it is a badly designed study.</p>
<p><strong>Theoretical factors</strong> are the page 4–5 debate applied. A positivist aim — establishing a pattern across a population — demands a standardised instrument and a large representative sample. An interpretivist aim — understanding a meaning from inside — demands open instruments and natural settings, and accepts a small sample as the price. And the hypothesis itself constrains the data: a causal claim needs controlled, measurable variables; a "what does it mean?" question is corrupted by being forced into ticks.</p>
<p>The last box of the diagram carries most of the marks in real answers: <strong>state the cost</strong>. Every choice buys one thing by giving up another — the survey trades depth for breadth; the observation the reverse — and an answer that names its own limitation reads as sociologically mature precisely because it has stopped treating method choice as a matter of taste. Page 14's worked example runs the whole frame on a live question; the practice here runs it in pieces.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Name the three families of factors that justify a method choice, with one example of each. (b) A researcher wants to study how homeless young people survive on the streets of Belfast. Suggest a method, justifying it from each family, and state what it costs. (c) Why can access be a practical factor that overrides a researcher's theoretical preference? (d) Explain why ethical factors make school-based research a special case.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Practical — time, money, skills, access, subject matter (e.g. a large survey needs months and funds a lone student lacks); ethical — consent, harm, privacy and the BSA Statement (e.g. covert observation of vulnerable groups needs special justification); theoretical — positivist/interpretivist fit and the hypothesis's data requirements (e.g. a pattern across a population demands a standardised instrument and a representative sample). (b) Method: participant observation and unstructured interviews through a day-centre, with snowball sampling. Practical: no sampling frame of street homeless exists, and the knowledge needed is experiential, so observation is the accessible route. Ethical: overt, via gatekeepers (centre staff), with consent, anonymity and referral routes — homeless young people are vulnerable, so no covert work. Theoretical: the question is a meaning question (how they survive), so validity is the aim and a small purposive sample is the accepted price. Cost: no generalisation to homeless young people in general and no replication; the researcher effect shadows everything reported. (c) Because a method can only run where its researcher is admitted: a study of gang life may have a strong interpretivist case for participant observation, but if no gate opens, a covert design is the only access — and ethics then restricts that too — so access decides what remains feasible whatever the theory prefers. (d) Because pupils are minors: informed consent is needed from parents and schools as well as from pupils themselves; the researcher must plan for safeguarding disclosures (harm to the child requires reporting, breaking ordinary confidentiality); and power differences between adult researcher and child respondent make genuinely voluntary consent harder to secure — so designs need age-appropriate information sheets, the right to withdraw, and data handling agreed with the school in advance.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 7, title: "The research cycle: from topic to findings and back",
content: `
<p>Pages 2 to 6 have built the parts; this page assembles the machine. The <strong>research process</strong> is a cycle, not a checklist, because each stage's output is the next stage's input, and the last stage feeds the first: findings answer the question that began the study, and raise the next one. The diagram is the map of every study you will evaluate in Lesson 11 — the WJEC research synopsis is, in effect, this cycle written in the past tense, and the evaluation questions are the cycle's stages asked in reverse.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/wjec-as-sociology/wjec-soc-research-cycle.png" alt="A loop diagram of the sociological research cycle with eight numbered stages. Left column, top to bottom: 1. The question — a puzzle worth studying, such as why working-class pupils on average leave school with less, turned into a question research can actually answer. 2. The background — what is already known: theory and earlier studies sharpen the question and suggest what to expect. 3. The hypothesis — a testable statement linking variables (for example, the longer a pupil is unemployed, the lower their reported life satisfaction) — or open questions for interpretivist work. 4. Operationalise — concepts become indicators become measures: class becomes occupation, religiosity becomes attendance and belief; the hypothesis can now be tested. Right column, bottom to top: 5. The pilot study — draft instruments go to a few cases first: ambiguous items, timing, refusals and question order are fixed before the real fieldwork. 6. Sample and fieldwork — choose population, sampling frame and sample, then collect the data under the ethics rules settled at the design stage. 7. Analysis — quantitative data yield patterns and comparisons; qualitative data yield meanings and themes; both are read back against the hypothesis. 8. Findings and report — answer the question and state the limits honestly; every answer raises the next question and the loop begins again. Arrows run down the left column, across the bottom, up the right column and back across the top to stage 1. Bottom band: ethics at every stage — not a hurdle at the end: consent and deception are settled at design, harm and privacy govern fieldwork, anonymity and confidentiality govern reporting; the BSA Statement of Ethical Practice applies to all of it; a study that cannot be conducted ethically is redesigned, not excused." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The research cycle. Note where the pilot study sits — <em>before</em> fieldwork, not after: its whole job is to catch the broken item before it reaches two thousand respondents. And note the band: ethics runs through every stage, which is why "it was approved by the ethics committee" is never a complete answer to an ethics question. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Three stages repay close reading. <strong>The background review</strong> (stage 2) is not bookishness: it is where the study finds out what is already known so that it asks something new, and where it finds validated instruments — existing questions, indices, classifications — that make its measures comparable with everything already published. A study that skips it rediscovers the wheel badly. <strong>The pilot study</strong> (stage 5) is the quality-control stage: the draft instrument goes to a handful of real cases, and every ambiguity, leading phrase, over-long interview and unexpected refusal is discovered while it is still cheap to fix. For interview schedules, pilots test the questions <em>and</em> the interviewer: the transcript of a pilot is the researcher's own mirror. <strong>Analysis</strong> (stage 7) is where quantitative and qualitative routes genuinely differ — patterns against meanings — but both end the same way, read back against the hypothesis, which is either supported, refuted (a result worth publishing too), or refined into a better hypothesis for the next turn of the cycle.</p>
<p>The cycle also explains a phrase you will meet throughout Unit 2: <strong>"the findings are only as good as the design."</strong> A weak sample cannot be rescued by clever analysis, and a broken measure cannot be repaired by a large sample — it just produces the same error more precisely. That is why the evaluation habit of Lesson 11 always starts at stage 1–4 of the cycle (question, design, measures, sample) and treats findings and conclusions as the <em>outputs</em> to be judged against them.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) List the eight stages of the research cycle in order. (b) Explain the two jobs of the background review. (c) A pilot study finds that respondents interpret the item "Do you exercise regularly?" in wildly different ways. Explain what the pilot has just saved the study from, and name the fix. (d) Why is "the findings are only as good as the design" the starting principle of evaluation?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) The question → the background → the hypothesis (or research questions) → operationalisation → the pilot study → sampling and fieldwork → analysis → findings and report; then the cycle reopens with a new question. (b) First, to establish what is already known so the study asks something genuinely new rather than duplicating existing work; second, to supply validated instruments and classifications — existing measures, indices and standard questions — that make the new study's data comparable with published findings. (c) It has saved a measurement-validity failure at scale: if the item had gone to the full sample, "regularly" would have been defined differently by different respondents, so the data would have measured the respondents' interpretations of the word rather than their exercise — irreparable after the event because the instrument is the same for everyone; the fix is to operationalise the item with a behavioural measure at the pilot stage (for example "On how many days in the last seven days did you take part in physical activity for at least 30 minutes?"), pretested on a new pilot. (d) Because findings are outputs of the stages before them: an unrepresentative sample cannot be corrected in analysis (the pattern describes only the sample), and a flawed measure reproduces its flaw at any scale — so evaluation asks whether the design could have produced trustworthy findings before it asks whether the findings sound plausible.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 8, title: "Populations, samples and sampling frames",
content: `
<p>Almost no study can study everyone, so almost every study must decide whom to study instead — and that decision is where representativeness is won or lost. The vocabulary must be exact, because the synopsis exercises test it line by line. The <strong>population</strong> is every member of the group the study is about: all sixth-formers in Northern Ireland, every pupil at one school, every mosque committee in Cardiff. The <strong>sample</strong> is the subset actually studied. The <strong>sampling frame</strong> is the list from which the sample can actually be drawn — the school's roll, the sixth-form register, the electoral register, a customer database. And a sample is <strong>representative</strong> to the extent that, on the variables that matter to the hypothesis, it resembles the population — so that patterns found in the sample can be generalised to the population.</p>
<p>Each definition carries a hidden trap, and the traps are examinable. The population is defined by the <em>research question</em>, not by convenience: a study whose hypothesis is about Northern Ireland's sixth-formers cannot generalise from a sample of one Belfast grammar school, however large, because the population and the frame never matched. The sampling frame is almost never the population: the electoral register misses people who are not registered; the school roll misses absentees on the day; an online panel misses everyone without broadband — and, crucially, the people missing from the frame are rarely missing at random. The young, the poor and the mobile are under-registered; a frame's blind spots are systematic, so a sample drawn from it inherits them. This is <strong>frame bias</strong>, and recognising it in a synopsis is a standard evaluation point.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>Sampling, exactly defined:</strong> population = every member of the group the study is about; sampling frame = the list it is actually possible to draw from; sample = the subset studied; representative = resembling the population on the variables that matter to the hypothesis. A perfect random sample of a bad frame is still biased — randomness operates within the frame, never beyond it.</p>
</div>
<p>Why sample at all? Because measuring everyone is usually impossible, always more expensive, and often less accurate than a good sample properly drawn. The alternative is <strong>frame bias's</strong> mirror image, <strong>sampling error</strong>: any sample differs from its population by chance, and the smaller the sample the larger the likely difference. This is not a flaw to be eliminated — it is a mathematical property to be managed, which is what probability sampling (page 9) is for, and why the honest findings section reports results with the sample size attached: "53% of the 1,200 pupils surveyed…", not "53% of pupils".</p>
<p>One more distinction organises the next two pages. A <strong>probability sample</strong> is drawn so that every member of the frame has a known, non-zero chance of selection — random, systematic, stratified. It exists to make samples representative <em>and</em> to let the researcher calculate how far off the sample is likely to be. A <strong>non-probability sample</strong> — quota, opportunity, snowball, purposive — does not give that guarantee, and is used where the frame does not exist (hidden populations) or the study's aim is depth rather than generalisation. Choosing between them is a design act, not a default: the wrong kind of sampling for the aim is one of the most frequent synopsis criticisms, and the next two pages give you the full toolkit.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Define population, sampling frame and sample, and explain what "representative" means. (b) A researcher samples 500 households from the Northern Ireland electoral register. Name two groups likely to be under-represented and explain why the under-representation matters. (c) Explain the difference between frame bias and sampling error. (d) Why is a probability sample impossible for a study of young carers in one city, and what follows for how the findings must be stated?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Population: every member of the group the research question is about; sampling frame: the list from which the sample can actually be drawn; sample: the subset actually studied; representative: resembling the population on the variables the hypothesis concerns (class, gender, age, attainment), so that patterns in the sample can be generalised to the population. (b) Groups missing or under-recorded on the electoral register include students and other highly mobile young adults, people in areas of low registration, and those disengaged from official processes; it matters because register-based sampling cannot select them at all, so they are absent from the sample by construction, not by chance — and if the study's variables (for example, community involvement or political attitudes) relate to registration itself, the findings are systematically distorted. (c) Frame bias is systematic under- or over-representation inherited from the list itself — it persists however large or random the sample, because randomness works only within the frame; sampling error is the chance difference between any sample and its population, which grows as samples shrink and which probability sampling allows the researcher to estimate — one is a design flaw, the other a manageable mathematical property. (d) Because probability sampling requires a frame listing every young carer, and no such register exists: young carers are a hidden population, unidentified even to schools, so no member can be given a known chance of selection; the study must therefore use non-probability methods (identifying carers through schools, support services and snowball referral), and its findings must be stated as about these cases — indicative, not generalisable to young carers as a whole.</p></div></details>` },
];